{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And He was appalled.</b> From the root of שממה (desolation) (Isaiah 1:7). And Rabbi Menahem, who derived it from the root of שתום העין (Numbers 24:3), did not speak correctly, for a person cannot say וישבור and וישבורר — [a verb from] a doubled lamed." "<b>And was saved to Him.</b> In human language, for HaShem is the savior and is not saved. The meaning concerns His glory, to show His might." "<b>To intercede.</b> Like ולפושעים יפגיע (and He intercedes for transgressors) (Isaiah 53:12)."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 59, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra's argument is compressed: he rejects deriving וישתומם from a biconsonantal root by analogy, since you cannot form the hitpa'el pattern from a doubled final root letter. The phrase כפול הלמ\"ד refers to a root with a doubled final radical."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:00:20.745865817Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}